.85</td><td align="center">0.65</td><td align="center">0.75</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Haplogroups as defined in Additional File <xref ref-type="supplementary-material" rid="S1">1</xref>. Superscripts refer to the reference number of the source data.</p><p>References: 1. Comas et al. (2004); 2. Quintana-Murci et al. (2004); 3. Yao et al. (2004)</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>NRY haplogroup frequencies and diversity values are presented in Table <xref ref-type="table" rid="T2">2</xref>. The highland Avar, Dargin, and Kubachi populations exhibit high frequencies of haplogroup F (including haplogroup G, see Discussion), which is also common in many Near Eastern populations. The lowland Nogai and Kumik samples exhibit a mixture of European, Near Eastern, and Central Asian haplogroups. The lowland Kumik and Nogai populations have NRY haplogroup diversity values as high as those observed in the Near East and Central Asia, while the highland Daghestani populations have lower NRY haplogroup diversity, less than or equal to that observed in our Central/Northern European sample. The highland Dargins are exceptional, with a Nei's <italic>h </italic>value of zero, as all males sampled belong to NRY haplogroup F.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Y-Chromosome Haplogroup Frequencies and Diversity.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td></td><td align="center"><bold>Avar</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Dargin</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Kubachi</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Kumik</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Nogai</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Crimean Tatar<sup>1</sup></bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Georgian<sup>2</sup></bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Iranian<sup>1</sup></bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Turkish<sup>2</sup></bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Karakalpak<sup>1</sup></bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Kazak<sup>1</sup></bold></td><td a